WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:07.110
A few weeks later, spring arrived. Every tree and flower put forth young leaves and rich blossoms.

2
00:00:07.111 --> 00:00:11.126
The Maylies and Oliver left Chertsey for a cottage in the country.

3
00:00:11.127 --> 00:00:18.118
It was a happy time. Every morning Oliver was up by six o'clock, roaming the fields in search of flowers.

4
00:00:18.119 --> 00:00:25.864
He returned home laden with blossoms for the breakfast table. Then he went to the village to study with a kind, white-haired gentleman.

5
00:00:25.865 --> 00:00:36.610
In the afternoon he walked with Mrs. Maylie and Rose. Sometimes they sat in a shady place and listened to Rose read aloud. After that Oliver prepared his lesson for the next day.

6
00:00:36.611 --> 00:00:42.428
In the evening they walked out again, and Oliver listened with great pleasure to all that the ladies said.

7
00:00:42.429 --> 00:00:49.111
When darkness fell and they returned home, Rose played the piano and sang in a gentle voice.

8
00:00:49.112 --> 00:00:53.269
So three months glided away and spring melted into summer.

9
00:00:53.270 --> 00:01:00.182
One beautiful night they had taken a longer walk than usual. When they returned home, Rose sat down at the piano.

10
00:01:00.183 --> 00:01:08.833
After running her hands over the keys, she began to play something solemn. And as she played, Oliver and Mrs. Maylie heard her weeping.

11
00:01:08.834 --> 00:01:16.230
"Rose, my love!" cried Mrs. Maylie, rising hastily and bending over her. "Why are you crying?"

12
00:01:16.231 --> 00:01:22.055
"I don't know," replied the young lady. "I can't describe it, but I feel . . ."

13
00:01:22.056 --> 00:01:24.563
"Not ill, my love?"

14
00:01:24.564 --> 00:01:32.396
"No, not ill." Rose shuddered with cold. "I'll be better soon. Please close the window."

15
00:01:32.397 --> 00:01:41.047
Oliver hurried to comply with her request. Rose tried to play a livelier tune, but her fingers fell powerless on the keys.

16
00:01:41.048 --> 00:01:45.619
Covering her face with her hands, she sank onto a sofa and wept.

17
00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:49.706
"I don't want to alarm you, Aunt, but I fear I am ill."

18
00:01:49.707 --> 00:01:54.611
Her face turned as white as marble, yet in another minute, it was flushed.

19
00:01:54.612 --> 00:02:01.532
Oliver was alarmed, but Mrs. Maylie tried to stay calm. She urged Rose to retire for the night.

20
00:02:01.533 --> 00:02:06.032
"I hope nothing is the matter," said Oliver when Rose had left the room.

21
00:02:06.033 --> 00:02:13.014
"I hope so too," said Mrs. Maylie. "But I fear I may be in danger of losing the dear girl."

22
00:02:13.015 --> 00:02:19.101
"Surely there is no danger of anything so dreadful? She was quite well two hours ago."

23
00:02:19.102 --> 00:02:27.484
"She's very ill now and will be worse, I'm sure. My dear, dear Rose. What would I do without her?"

24
00:02:27.484 --> 00:02:33.347
Oliver did not know what to say. He could not bear to think of Rose dying.

25
00:02:33.348 --> 00:02:39.164
An anxious night passed. The next morning Rose had a high and dangerous fever.

26
00:02:39.165 --> 00:02:48.410
"We must take action, Oliver." Mrs. Maylie handed him a letter for Dr. Losberne. "This letter must be carried to the market town, four miles away.

27
00:02:48.411 --> 00:02:54.831
Then it should be taken by horseback to Chertsey. The people at the inn will know how to do this."

28
00:02:54.832 --> 00:02:57.395
Oliver put the letter in his pocket.

29
00:02:57.396 --> 00:03:06.411
"Here's another letter," said Mrs. Maylie, hesitating a bit. "But I don't know whether to send it now or wait until I see if Rose worsens."

30
00:03:06.412 --> 00:03:11.403
"Is it for Chertsey too, ma'am?" Oliver held out his trembling hand.

31
00:03:11.404 --> 00:03:15.546
"No," replied Mrs. Maylie, giving it to him mechanically.

32
00:03:15.547 --> 00:03:21.800
Oliver glanced at the envelope and saw it was addressed to Harry Maylie at some great lord's house.

33
00:03:21.801 --> 00:03:24.943
"Shall it go too?" Oliver asked.

34
00:03:24.944 --> 00:03:31.459
"I think not," replied Mrs. Maylie, taking back the letter. "I'll wait until tomorrow."

35
00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:39.538
With these words she gave Oliver her purse, and he started off. Swiftly he ran across the fields and down the little lanes.

36
00:03:39.539 --> 00:03:43.292
Now and then he stopped for a few seconds to catch his breath.

37
00:03:43.293 --> 00:03:47.943
At last he arrived, hot and covered with dust, at the market town.

38
00:03:47.944 --> 00:03:54.935
Looking around, he spotted the large house that was the inn. He spoke to a boy, who had been dozing at the gate.

39
00:03:54.936 --> 00:03:59.252
The boy referred him to the landlord, who was leaning against the stable door.

40
00:03:59.253 --> 00:04:05.848
The landlord walked slowly into the inn to make out a bill for delivering the letter, and he was gone a long time.

41
00:04:05.849 --> 00:04:13.681
After the bill was ready and Oliver had paid it with the money from Mrs. Maylie's purse, a horse had to be saddled and a man had to get ready.

42
00:04:13.682 --> 00:04:18.165
Meanwhile Oliver was in a desperate state of impatience and anxiety.

43
00:04:18.166 --> 00:04:22.316
If he could, he would have jumped on the horse himself and ridden away.

44
00:04:22.317 --> 00:04:28.221
At last the letter was on its way, and Oliver hurried out to the innyard with a somewhat lighter heart.

45
00:04:28.222 --> 00:04:33.832
He stumbled against a tall man, wrapped in a dark cloak, who was coming out the inn door.

46
00:04:33.833 --> 00:04:40.062
"Hah! What's this?" cried the man, fixing his eyes on Oliver and jumping back in surprise.

47
00:04:40.063 --> 00:04:46.158
"I beg your pardon, sir," said Oliver. "I was in a great hurry to get home and didn't see you."

48
00:04:46.159 --> 00:04:50.659
"Death to you!" cried the man in a horrible passion.

49
00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:57.047
"If only I'd had the courage to say the word, I could have been free of you. What are you doing here?"

50
00:04:57.047 --> 00:05:02.792
The man shook his fist at Oliver and advanced toward him as if he wanted to strike the startled boy.

51
00:05:02.793 --> 00:05:07.524
But before that could happen, the man fell violently to the ground, writhing in a fit.

52
00:05:07.525 --> 00:05:10.381
Oliver darted into the inn for help.

53
00:05:10.381 --> 00:05:19.793
Once the man was carried safely inside, Oliver ran as fast as he could to make up for lost time. All the way home, he thought of the strange, menacing man.

54
00:05:19.794 --> 00:05:26.023
But once he reached the cottage, there were more pressing things to think about. Rose had grown worse.

55
00:05:26.024 --> 00:05:30.790
In fact, a local medical practitioner said it would be a miracle if she recovered.

56
00:05:30.791 --> 00:05:35.846
The next day visitors came and went, speaking in hushed voices.

57
00:05:35.847 --> 00:05:39.270
Late at night Dr. Losberne finally arrived.

58
00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:42.723
"There is very little hope," he announced after examining Rose.

59
00:05:42.724 --> 00:05:44.929
Oliver's heart sank.

60
00:05:44.929 --> 00:05:53.087
The next morning Oliver went out to walk in the fields. It was a beautiful, sunny day with a sense of peace and beauty in the landscape.

61
00:05:53.088 --> 00:05:58.341
Oliver turned homeward, thinking of Rose and the many kindnesses he had received from her.

62
00:05:58.342 --> 00:06:02.015
When he reached home, Mrs. Maylie was sitting in the parlor.

63
00:06:02.016 --> 00:06:10.912
"She is in a deep sleep, Oliver," said the grieving old woman. "Dr. Losberne says she will either recover or never wake up again."

64
00:06:10.913 --> 00:06:19.983
Oliver sat down next to Mrs. Maylie on the sofa. He held her hand, and there they sat for hours, refusing all food and afraid to speak.

65
00:06:19.984 --> 00:06:25.278
Together they watched as the sun sank lower and lower.

